WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:05.609
In July 2017, Milana Vayntrub was cast as
Marvel superhero Squirrel Girl in the new

00:00:05.609 --> 00:00:08.330
Freeform comedy series, New Warriors.

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:13.000
And while you might not recognize the actress's
name, her face should be familiar.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.740
From TV roles to web series, plus a certain
gig with AT&amp;T, here are all the places you've

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:22.220
previously seen future Squirrel Girl Milana
Vayntrub.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:29.369
"Drop the money, scuzz-bucket, or face the
wrath of Squirrel Girl!"

00:00:29.369 --> 00:00:30.970
A start on television

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:35.710
Vayntrub landed her first memorable TV role
as a child, playing an HIV-positive Russian

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:39.030
girl named Tatiana on the classic hospital
drama ER in 1995.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:44.690
Carol, played by future Good Wife star Julianna
Margulies, wanted to adopt the girl, but it

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:48.010
didn't work out, and Vayntrub left the show
after three episodes.

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:51.710
From there, she went on to small roles in
Days of our Lives and Lizzie McGuire — but

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:54.410
once the Internet came into play, things got
more interesting.

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:58.170
"How do you like, h-how do you, H-h-how do
you like them apples?"

00:00:58.170 --> 00:00:58.760
"Uh."

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:01.219
An online star

00:01:01.219 --> 00:01:05.489
Milana Vayntrub's most famous work online
came as the costar of the internet series,

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:07.890
"Let's Talk About Something More Interesting."

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:12.610
Along with fellow actress Stevie Nelson, the
2011 series featured Vayntrub conducting intentionally

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:15.690
terrible interviews with celebrities like
Matt Damon.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:16.840
"Hi, hey."

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:20.970
This was also when Vayntrub started appearing
in College Humor projects, including the "Jake

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:25.030
and Amir" series, as well as a famous public
service announcement about Bitchy Resting

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:26.030
Face.

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:29.040
"That might just be her bitchface, and that’s
okay!"

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:31.420
AT&amp;T

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:35.560
In 2013, Milana Vayntrub scored the role that
would define her career for years to come:

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:39.909
Lily, the friendly and patient wireless store
clerk in a popular series of commercials for

00:01:39.909 --> 00:01:40.909
AT&amp;T.

00:01:40.909 --> 00:01:44.810
Lily is a little awkward, lightly sarcastic,
and occasionally flirtatious.

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:48.170
She's more girl next door than spokesmodel,
like someone you might actually encounter

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:51.509
working in an AT&amp;T store…well, if you were
lucky.

00:01:51.509 --> 00:01:56.200
As Lily, Vayntrub has shared scenes with regular
wireless customers, as well as celebrities

00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:00.590
including Gordon Ramsay and James Van Der
Beek — and her natural charisma was surely

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:02.579
the key to the campaign's long run.

00:02:02.579 --> 00:02:06.470
"You should negotiate more stuff, you're pretty
good at it."

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:07.470
Key and Peele

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:12.280
In a role as different from her perky AT&amp;T
persona as possible, Vayntrub made a mostly

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:17.129
silent but highly memorable appearance on
the 2013 Halloween episode of Comedy Central's

00:02:17.129 --> 00:02:18.860
sketch series Key and Peele.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:22.959
She's nearly unrecognizable asthe gorgeous
vampire girl hanging on the arm of Jordan

00:02:22.959 --> 00:02:27.120
Peele's gothic lord of the undead, who gets
called out by a new member of the vampire

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.529
gang for making mealtime weird.

00:02:28.529 --> 00:02:31.730
"I'm just saying, y'all over here licking
and hissing and whatnot.

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:34.220
If we gon' eat, then let's eat, damn."

00:02:35.689 --> 00:02:39.620
Although if you stick around to see the vampires
get a makeover at the end of the sketch, Vayntrub

00:02:39.629 --> 00:02:42.159
looks a little bit more like herself.

00:02:42.159 --> 00:02:43.379
House of Lies

00:02:43.379 --> 00:02:48.249
In 2014, Vayntrub appeared in the Showtime
series House of Lies, in which she plays Christy,

00:02:48.249 --> 00:02:52.919
a consulting firm employee who's both fired
and arrested after stabbing her boss Monica

00:02:52.919 --> 00:02:54.989
Talbot, played by Dawn Olivieri.

00:02:54.989 --> 00:02:59.029
Although she only appears in two episodes,
her memorable performance showcased yet another

00:02:59.029 --> 00:03:03.579
very different kind of character from the
one she played in those AT&amp;T ads.

00:03:03.579 --> 00:03:04.980
Silicon Valley

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.189
You also might have spotted Vayntrub on HBO's
Silicon Valley.

00:03:08.189 --> 00:03:12.810
Her character, Tara, is the girlfriend of
Martin Starr's cynical Bertram Gilfoyle — and

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:16.840
like Bertram, Tara is a practicing LaVeyan
Satanist.

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:17.999
Other Space

00:03:17.999 --> 00:03:22.769
Created by Ghostbusters director Paul Feig,
this 2015 series was part of the launch of

00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:25.089
Yahoo's streaming service, Yahoo Screen.

00:03:25.089 --> 00:03:30.030
Vayntrub starred as Tina Shukshin, a flighty
and unpredictable starship navigator.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:31.779
Unfortunately, Yahoo!

00:03:31.779 --> 00:03:35.589
Screen failed to generate profits for the
struggling company, and the whole thing was

00:03:35.589 --> 00:03:37.920
shut down in early 2016.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:41.520
"That's the spirit."

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:42.980
Love

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:47.670
Vayntrub also had a crucial if short-lived
role in the first two episodes of Love, the

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:49.430
Netflix original series.

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:53.849
She plays Natalie, whose messy breakup with
Gus, played by Paul Rust, sets the events

00:03:53.849 --> 00:03:58.200
of the story in motion that lead him to a
happier, healthier relationship.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:03:59.529
Can't Do Nothing

00:03:59.529 --> 00:04:04.689
Before she was an actress, Milana Vayntrub
was actually a refugee, having fled the USSR

00:04:04.689 --> 00:04:06.919
with her parents in the 1980s.

00:04:06.919 --> 00:04:12.790
"The Soviet Union is finally allowing thousands
of Jews to emigrate flee their lives of religious

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:13.480
persecution."

00:04:17.789 --> 00:04:22.139
With her status as a celebrity, Vayntrub found
herself in a position to help other refugees

00:04:22.139 --> 00:04:23.310
around the world.

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:28.500
In 2016 she founded Can't Do Nothing, a charity
dedicated to helping displaced people from

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:30.189
Syria and elsewhere.

00:04:30.189 --> 00:04:34.789
Amid a turbulent presidential election in
which the refugee crisis was an ongoing issue,

00:04:34.789 --> 00:04:39.110
it made news that "the AT&amp;T girl" was involved
on a personal level.

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:40.229
This Is Us

00:04:40.229 --> 00:04:42.590
Milana Vayntrub's most visible role to date?

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:46.720
It's her eight-episode arc on the massively
successful NBC drama This Is Us.

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:50.710
She played Sloane Sandburg, a playwright and
romantic interest for Justin Hartley's Kevin

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:51.710
Pearson.

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:55.389
Sadly, because the show is essentially a soap
opera, their relationship ended in dramatic

00:04:55.389 --> 00:04:58.660
fashion — as did Vayntrub's work on the
show.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:00.720
"It's the right thing to do."

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:09.300
But high-profile guest roles like this one
lead to bigger things, and it's not surprising

00:05:09.319 --> 00:05:11.720
to see Milana Vayntrub move on to New Warriors.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:16.310
Sure, right now it feels like she'll always
be Lily from those AT&amp;T commercials — but

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:20.030
soon, Squirrel Girl might be the reason why
everyone knows Vayntrub's name.

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:24.310
Thanks for watching!

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:26.849
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:26.849 --> 00:05:29.190
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

